  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "If the $6$th term of an arithmetic series is $\\frac{37}{4}$ and the sum of the first $16$ terms is $228,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We are given one term of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=16$ and ${S}_{N}=228$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 228 & =\\frac{16}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(16-1)d) \\\\ 228 & =8(2{a}_{1}+15d) \\\\ \\frac{57}{2} & =2{a}_{1}+15d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we can write an equation for the $6$th term of the arithmetic series and substitute in its known value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}+(6-1)d \\\\ \\frac{37}{4} & ={a}_{1}+5d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{4}={a}_{1}+5d \\\\ \\frac{57}{2}=2{a}_{1}+15d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and add the result to the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{37}{2} & =-2{a}_{1}-10d \\\\ \\frac{57}{2} & =2{a}_{1}+15d \\\\ \\frac{20}{2} & =5d \\\\ d & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $d=2$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{4} & ={a}_{1}+5d \\\\ \\frac{37}{4} & ={a}_{1}+5(2) \\\\ \\frac{37}{4} & ={a}_{1}+\\frac{40}{4} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
